Summary: Rosetta 2: make it notice code written into another process
Category: cpu
Kind: workaround
Origin: crossover
  Version: 26.3.0
  CW-Hacks: 18947
  URL: https://media.codeweavers.com/pub/crossover/source/crossover-sources-26.3.0.tar.gz
  Files: dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
  Note: Sikarugir 10.0_8 ships it: its NtWriteVirtualMemory calls
    NtQueryVirtualMemory and NtProtectVirtualMemory twice after the server
    call (disassembly; no strings of its own, so the string inventory
    missed it)
  Changes: decides region by region from each region's current protection,
    not the first region's AllocationProtect, and skips the toggle when the
    write failed; is_apple_silicon() is in ntdll-is-apple-silicon
Upstream: no
Why: NtWriteVirtualMemory writes another process's memory with
 mach_vm_write from wineserver, and Rosetta doesn't notice code changed
 that way: the target keeps running its old translation. After a write
 that succeeded, walk the written range region by region, and for each
 region whose current protection has execute rights, change that part's
 protection to PAGE_NOACCESS and back from inside the target
 (NtProtectVirtualMemory on the process handle), so Rosetta retranslates.
 Armadillo, the DRM in Big Fish games, writes "jmp $" (EB FE) over its
 child's entry point, waits for the child to reach it, then writes the
 original bytes back; without this the child spins on the stale "jmp $"
 for ever and activation fails. Toggling the entry page's protection by
 hand inside a stuck gmActivator child (lldb, mprotect) let it continue
 as on Sikarugir 10.0_8 (2026-11-15). CrossOver's version decides from the
 first region's AllocationProtect, which is PAGE_EXECUTE_WRITECOPY for
 every page of an image, so a write into an image's data made the page
 PAGE_NOACCESS for a moment and killed the target when another thread
 touched it (a child written every 5 ms died after 4-5 writes, 12 of 12).
Programs: Big Fish games (Armadillo DRM): activation fails, as gmActivator's
 child spins for ever on a stale "jmp $"
Seen on: macOS 26.7.2 (M1 Max): cross-process-code-write fails 3 of 3 with
 the toggle turned off (an interposer hiding sysctl.proc_translated)
Uncertain: a thread of the target that runs code on a page while it's
 being toggled can still fault (inherent to toggling; toggling to the same
 protection minus execute instead would narrow it, but whether that still
 reaches Rosetta in a 32-bit process with DEP off, where Wine keeps
 PROT_EXEC on every readable page, isn't checked)
Expects before it: ntdll-is-apple-silicon; is_apple_silicon(), split out of
 this patch's CW HACK 18947 (2026-11-26)
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-26: toggles only regions whose current protection
 has execute rights, region by region, and only after a write that
 succeeded; macfixes 11.22 quick20261126T002454 passed cross-process-data-write
 and cross-process-code-write, i686 and x86_64 (macOS 26.7.2, M1 Max)
 11.22: 2026-11-26: quick builds 20261126T002454 and T025644 (macOS 26.7.2,
 M1 Max): smoke and the regression suite passed

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -6689,6 +6689,52 @@
     }
 
     return apple_silicon_status;
+}
+#endif
+
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+/* CW HACK 18947
+ * If mach_vm_write() is used to modify code cross-process (which is how we implement
+ * NtWriteVirtualMemory), Rosetta won't notice the change and will execute the "old" code.
+ *
+ * To work around this, after the write completes,
+ * toggle the protection (from inside the target process) off and back on for any
+ * executable pages that were modified, to force Rosetta to re-translate them.
+ *
+ * wine-mac-builds: decide region by region from each region's current protection.
+ * CrossOver's version looks only at the first region's AllocationProtect, which is
+ * PAGE_EXECUTE_WRITECOPY for every page of an image, so a write into an image's data
+ * made the page PAGE_NOACCESS for a moment, and the target's other threads faulted.
+ */
+static void toggle_executable_pages_for_rosetta( HANDLE process, void *addr, SIZE_T size )
+{
+    MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION info;
+    char *ptr = addr, *end = (char *)addr + size;
+    SIZE_T ret;
+
+    if (!is_apple_silicon())
+        return;
+
+    while (ptr < end)
+    {
+        char *region_end;
+
+        if (NtQueryVirtualMemory( process, ptr, MemoryBasicInformation, &info, sizeof(info), &ret ))
+            break;
+        region_end = (char *)info.BaseAddress + info.RegionSize;
+        if (region_end <= ptr) break;
+
+        if (info.State == MEM_COMMIT && (info.Protect & 0xf0))
+        {
+            void *base = ptr;
+            SIZE_T len = (region_end < end ? region_end : end) - ptr;
+            ULONG origprot, prot;
+
+            if (!NtProtectVirtualMemory( process, &base, &len, PAGE_NOACCESS, &origprot ))
+                NtProtectVirtualMemory( process, &base, &len, origprot, &prot );
+        }
+        ptr = region_end;
+    }
 }
 #endif
 
@@ -6712,6 +6758,10 @@
             size = reply->written;
         }
         SERVER_END_REQ;
+
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+        if (!status) toggle_executable_pages_for_rosetta( process, addr, size );
+#endif
     }
     else
     {
